Investigate this Wazuh alert:

{
  "severity": "critical",
  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "185.220.101.91", "inferred_role": "external_attacker_controlled_resolver_and_proxy_target", "first_seen": "2027-12-04T08:08:42+0100", "last_seen": "2027-12-04T08:24:42+0100"},
    {"ip": "10.30.7.42", "inferred_role": "internal_workstation_resolving_hijacked_subdomain", "first_seen": "2027-12-04T08:14:42+0100", "last_seen": "2027-12-04T08:15:42+0100", "agent_name": "WS-FIN-09"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Resource Development / Compromise Infrastructure (Domains)", "technique": "T1584.001", "what_happened": "At 2027-12-04 08:08:42 CET, Suricata's external DNS-monitoring sensor logged that the authoritative DNS for corp.com started returning NEW nameserver (NS) records on the public internet. Prior to this point, the corp.com zone delegation pointed to ns1.corp.dns.com and ns2.corp.dns.com (the registrar-managed nameservers). After 08:08:42, queries to the root/TLD servers for corp.com began returning ns1.evilregistrar.net and ns2.evilregistrar.net as the delegated nameservers. This is a registrar-account-side modification: someone with control of the corp.com registrar portal (Cloudflare / GoDaddy / Markmonitor / similar) changed the delegated NS records. The infrastructure-compromise root cause is the registrar account (likely via phished registrar credentials, MFA bypass, social-engineered helpdesk transfer, or a registrar-side breach).", "chain_reason": "Suricata external-DNS-monitor signature 'EXTERNAL DNS NS record change for monitored domain' on data.alert.signature contains 'NS record change' AND on the corp.com zone. Univariate signature: monitored domain + NS RDATA changed compared to last-known-good snapshot.", "first_seen": "2027-12-04T08:08:42+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Infrastructure Tamper (Decoder Confirmation)", "technique": "T1584.001", "what_happened": "At 08:11:18 CET, the Wazuh decoder for external-DNS-change events fired a CRITICAL alert: 'EXTERNAL DNS — corp.com NS records changed from ns1.corp.dns.com / ns2.corp.dns.com (registrar-managed) to ns1.evilregistrar.net / ns2.evilregistrar.net (attacker-controlled)'. The decoder runs on a snapshot-diff schedule (compares the latest authoritative NS records against the last-known-good snapshot stored in Wazuh state). The 2 minutes 36 seconds between the Suricata DNS observation and the decoder alert is the snapshot-poll latency, NOT defender response time. By this point the hijack is already PROPAGATED at the TLD level — anyone resolving corp.com or any *.corp.com subdomain from anywhere on the internet now gets pointed to the attacker's nameservers.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh decoder rule for 'monitored domain NS RDATA delta' on the corp.com domain. The decoder reads from Suricata's external-DNS-monitor index and emits a structured alert with old_ns + new_ns fields. Linkage: domain=corp.com + ns_change_detected=true.", "first_seen": "2027-12-04T08:11:18+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Initial Access / Drive-By Redirection (Internal Resolution to Attacker IP)", "technique": "T1584.001 + T1566.002", "what_happened": "At 08:14:42 CET, Suricata DNS-log observed an internal workstation WS-FIN-09 (10.30.7.42) issue a DNS query for m365.corp.com (the company's internal Microsoft 365 sign-in bridge URL, normally CNAMEd to a corp-managed redirect that lands users at login.microsoftonline.com). The answer returned was 185.220.101.91 — an external attacker IP, NOT the legitimate Microsoft 365 redirect target. Because the corp.com zone is now served by attacker-controlled nameservers (ns1.evilregistrar.net / ns2.evilregistrar.net), every *.corp.com subdomain resolution is under attacker control. Users who reach m365.corp.com (typically via a corporate bookmark, a Single Sign-On link in an internal portal, or an email-sent Outlook-Web link) will land on a fake Microsoft 365 sign-in page hosted at 185.220.101.91, type their credentials, and have them phished.", "chain_reason": "Suricata DNS-log: data.src_ip=10.30.7.42 + queryName=m365.corp.com + answer 185.220.101.91. The answer IP does NOT match the known-legitimate m365.corp.com target (login.microsoftonline.com bridge). Linkage: queryName + answer IP + source IP.", "first_seen": "2027-12-04T08:14:42+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Initial Access / Sysmon Endpoint Confirmation", "technique": "T1584.001 + T1566.002", "what_happened": "At 08:15:08 CET (~26 seconds after the Suricata DNS observation), Sysmon Event 22 ('DNS query') fired on WS-FIN-09.corp.local: queryName=m365.corp.com, queryResults=185.220.101.91. This is endpoint-side confirmation of the same hijacked resolution observed by Suricata in step 3, with the endpoint clock + endpoint process visibility (Sysmon Event 22 includes Image = the process that issued the query, which for HTTPS-via-browser is typically chrome.exe / msedge.exe / firefox.exe). The 26-second offset is normal endpoint-vs-network-sensor clock skew + Sysmon-to-Wazuh-pipeline latency.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 22 on agent.name=WS-FIN-09.corp.local with data.win.eventdata.queryName=m365.corp.com and data.win.eventdata.queryResults=185.220.101.91. Linkage to step 3: same workstation + same queryName + same answer IP within a 26-second endpoint-vs-network correlation window.", "first_seen": "2027-12-04T08:15:08+0100"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / TLS Cert Mismatch (Hijack-Specific Smoking Gun)", "technique": "T1584.001 + T1557", "what_happened": "At 08:15:42 CET, Suricata TLS-log fired a 'TLS certificate name mismatch' alert: src=10.30.7.42 (WS-FIN-09) → dest=185.220.101.91:443. The attacker proxy presented a TLS certificate with CN/SAN=*.attackerproxy.net (issued by Let's Encrypt, only 5 days old), but the client SNI (Server Name Indication) requested m365.corp.com. A legitimate m365.corp.com endpoint would present a cert with CN/SAN matching corp.com or login.microsoftonline.com. The cert-mismatch is the CLEAREST possible hijack signal — it proves not just that DNS resolved wrong (which could be a misconfiguration), but that the host at the resolved IP is impersonating the corp.com brand with an attacker-controlled certificate. ANY browser with HSTS or modern TLS warnings would flash a cert-error to the user; many users click through, but the SIEM-side detection is unambiguous.", "chain_reason": "Suricata TLS-log signature on data.alert.signature contains 'TLS certificate name mismatch' OR 'cert CN does not match SNI'. data.src_ip=10.30.7.42 + data.dest_ip=185.220.101.91 + data.dest_port=443 + cert_subject_cn=*.attackerproxy.net + tls_sni=m365.corp.com. Linkage: same workstation + same destination IP + cert vs SNI string delta.", "first_seen": "2027-12-04T08:15:42+0100"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Resource Development / Hijack Scope Expansion", "technique": "T1584.001", "what_happened": "At 08:18:18 CET, the Wazuh decoder fired a follow-up ALERT: 'ALL *.corp.com subdomain resolutions now point to a single attacker IP (185.220.101.91)'. The decoder ran a post-hijack scope assessment query against Suricata DNS-monitor data for the past 10 minutes and confirmed every observed *.corp.com lookup (mail.corp.com, vpn.corp.com, sso.corp.com, portal.corp.com, m365.corp.com, owa.corp.com — the standard corporate subdomains) now resolves to 185.220.101.91. This is the worst-case scope: a single attacker IP is harvesting credentials for every corporate-branded service simultaneously, with the user-side trust signal being 'I typed the corporate URL into my browser, the corporate-name bookmark works' — fake portals are the natural landing pages.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh decoder follow-up rule: aggregation over data.win.eventdata.queryName (or Suricata DNS queryName) matching the corp.com domain, group by queryName, check the answer IP set. When the answer IP set collapses to a single non-corp-owned external IP, fire CRITICAL scope-expansion alert. Linkage: domain=corp.com + answer_ip_cardinality=1 + answer_ip NOT IN corp_owned_cidr.", "first_seen": "2027-12-04T08:18:18+0100"},
    {"step": 7, "tactic": "Credential Access / Mass Credential Harvesting via Hijacked Portals", "technique": "T1566.002 + T1598", "what_happened": "At 08:24:42 CET, Wazuh's O365 audit-log integration (which reads Microsoft 365 sign-in logs from the LEGITIMATE Microsoft side, NOT from the hijacked corp DNS) shows a normal mix of legitimate user logins continuing through real login.microsoftonline.com — these are users who typed direct URLs or used corporate-managed device SSO. However, the SOC's correlation engine flags a divergence: multiple users who normally hit m365.corp.com bookmarks during the 08:14-08:24 window did NOT appear in the legitimate O365 sign-in logs during that window. That means those users hit the attacker's fake portal at 185.220.101.91, typed their credentials there, and have been credential-harvested. Every user who hit any *.corp.com subdomain via the corp DNS during the hijack window is presumed credentials-compromised.", "chain_reason": "Cross-source correlation: (a) Suricata DNS-log for *.corp.com resolutions during 08:08-08:24 window (list of internal source IPs that resolved hijacked subdomains), (b) Microsoft O365 audit-log for the SAME usernames during the SAME window (expected to show legitimate sign-ins). Users whose workstation resolved m365.corp.com (or sso.corp.com etc) but whose username does NOT appear in legitimate O365 sign-in logs during the window = presumed phished. Linkage: workstation-to-user mapping (AD lookup) + DNS-resolution-observed + O365-sign-in-absence.", "first_seen": "2027-12-04T08:24:42+0100"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2027-12-04T08:08:42+0100", "end": "2027-12-04T08:24:42+0100"},
  "alert_count": 11,
  "unique_steps": 7
}
